// docs/spec/en/07-constexpr-and-consteval.md (ch09) §9.1(4): every
// potentially-evaluated call to an immediate function shall produce a
// constant expression. Inside an immediate function context that requirement
// is already met by the enclosing evaluation ([expr.const.imm]/1, adopted
// unchanged via §9.1(1)/(2) and front-matter §1(2)), so a receiver that is an
// ordinary local *reference* is admissible: the reference is bound during the
// same constant evaluation that performs the member call.
// docs/spec/en/06-constant-evaluation.md (ch06) §7.2(5.2) requires local
// declarations to be supported during required constant evaluation, and
// §7.2(6.2) requires the `consteval` calls themselves.
//
// Call position: inside another `consteval` function. Callee: `consteval`
// non-static member function. Receiver: a reference.
//
// Expected value derived from the spec text above and independently confirmed
// with `clang++-22 -std=c++26`, which compiles this exact source and exits 42.
//
// CURRENT STATUS (scpp @ a59ffce): FAILS. scpp reports "error: references,
// functions and function pointers are not supported during constant
// evaluation". §7.2(5.2) lists "local declarations" among the constructs the
// implementation shall support during required constant evaluation, and §7.3
// does not list reference binding among the unsupported operations, so
// §7.1(3)'s "permitted by 7.2" test is met. (Reported alongside this case:
// §7.2(1)'s constexpr-compatible list omits reference types, which may be the
// source of the disagreement -- if the intent really is to exclude
// references, §7.2/§7.3 need to say so.) The expectation above comes from the
// spec and from clang; do not weaken it to match the implementation.
class Counter {
public:
    virtual ~Counter() = default;
public:
    int value{};

    consteval Counter(int v) {
        this->value = v;
        return;
    }

    consteval int doubled() const {
        return this->value * 2;
    }
};

consteval int compute() {
    Counter c{21};
    const Counter& alias = c;
    return alias.doubled();
}

int main() {
    return compute();
}
